. *****************************************************************************
> ********
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 * 
. 
. /*
> We estimate a pooled Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression model to examine
>  whether having a daughter as the first child is associated with greater gend
> er equality within the household. The dependent variable is a ratio measuring
>  the distribution of unpaid domestic work (housework and childcare) within co
> habiting couples. This index is defined as the ratio of the smaller to the la
> rger number of hours reported by each partner on these tasks and ranges from 
> 0 (complete inequality) to 1 (perfect equality). Higher values reflect a more
>  balanced division of responsibilities.
> 
> The analysis is restricted to cohabiting couples observed between 2000 and 20
> 20 who are jointly listed as the biological parents of the same first child, 
> live in private households, are between 18 and 65 years old, and do not repor
> t same-sex partnerships or registered disabilities. We exclude couples whose 
> first birth resulted in twins or higher-order multiples. These restrictions e
> nsure that the transition to parenthood was experienced jointly and that obse
> rved differences in household labor division are not confounded by disability
>  status or unrelated prior family structures. We further exclude cases with m
> issing or invalid values for hours of domestic work.
> 
> The key independent variable is d_daughter, a binary indicator equal to 1 if 
> the couple’s first biological child is a daughter born on or before the inter
> view date, and 0 if the first child is a son. The model includes a rich set o
> f controls: the mother’s and father’s age and age squared; indicators for whe
> ther both partners have the same level of education and the highest level of 
> education within the couple; a dummy for being married; a dummy for both part
> ners being employed; a dummy for whether the mother is the primary earner; th
> e logarithm of net household income; dummies for whether both partners were b
> orn in Germany or not; and indicators for the number of children in the house
> hold within the age brackets 0–4, 5–9, and 10–14.
> 
> The model controls for year fixed effects, and standard errors are clustered 
> at the household level. A positive and statistically significant coefficient 
> on d_daughter (β₁ > 0, p < 0.05) will be interpreted as evidence supporting t
> he hypothesis that having a daughter is associated with greater gender equali
> ty in the division of unpaid domestic work. We report the coefficient estimat
> e, its t-statistic, the corresponding p-value, and the 95% confidence interva
> l.
> */
.         
.         
.         
. global controls "age_m age_sqrt_m age_f age_sqrt_f i.married i.sameeduc edu_h
> igh_couple edu_tertiary_couple log_hinc both_germborn none_germborn both_east
>  none_east both_employed male_bw female_bw child_1to4 child_5to9 child_10to14
> "

. 
. reghdfe DW_equal d_daughter $controls, a(i.syear) vce(cluster hid)
(MWFE estimator converged in 1 iterations)
note: none_germborn omitted because of collinearity
note: none_east omitted because of collinearity

